1
00:00:22,113 --> 00:00:25,216
[SPEAKER_00]: You mentioned the other day about senses and sense objects.

2
00:00:27,178 --> 00:00:30,380
[SPEAKER_00]: And you asked if... can you be disturbed?

3
00:00:33,683 --> 00:00:38,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there any other... can you be disturbed if there wasn't sense objects?

4
00:00:39,108 --> 00:00:47,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there any form of emotional disturbance that doesn't involve... doesn't involve the sense objects or senses?

5
00:00:48,746 --> 00:01:02,094
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is no... You can't experience anything else other than senses, sense objects, from that point of view.

6
00:01:04,956 --> 00:01:08,418
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't... That's all you know.

7
00:01:11,119 --> 00:01:12,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yeah, yeah, sure.

8
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:13,921
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your mode of being, yeah.

9
00:01:13,941 --> 00:01:15,342
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why kamabhāva,

10
00:01:17,994 --> 00:01:24,597
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a being that arises only when you engage with sensuality.

11
00:01:25,597 --> 00:01:34,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when the Buddha talks about three types of being, you should think of them as three types of threshold that your mind might be living on.

12
00:01:36,402 --> 00:01:37,683
[SPEAKER_01]: So, three types of domain.

13
00:01:40,778 --> 00:01:47,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have the central being, is the most most common one, that's the most, the broadest one, that's what everybody is in.

14
00:01:48,740 --> 00:01:59,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you have the rūpa, rūpa-bhāva and arūpa-bhāva, kama-bhāva, rūpa-bhāva, arūpa-bhāva.

15
00:01:59,762 --> 00:02:05,884
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, these three types of being that the Buddha talks about, three types of bhāva,

16
00:02:08,152 --> 00:02:24,002
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, as you said, sometimes people translate it as becoming, which, again, sort of comes and plays into that view as if only when you are engaging with karma, centrality, that being is becoming.

17
00:02:24,582 --> 00:02:26,624
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you stop engaging, that being is not becoming.

18
00:02:26,664 --> 00:02:26,804
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

19
00:02:27,304 --> 00:02:34,549
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be restrained for the rest of your life and not engage with centrality and still be within the central domain.

20
00:02:35,321 --> 00:02:37,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Within the domain of the sensual bhāva.

21
00:02:37,983 --> 00:02:45,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're not engaging with chasing the pleasures, your mind is still on the level of... of... on the threshold of senses and sense objects.

22
00:02:45,689 --> 00:02:48,231
[SPEAKER_01]: It hasn't... hasn't developed beyond that.

23
00:02:48,331 --> 00:02:53,275
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your mind is resting either on the coarsest, which is sensual domain,

24
00:02:55,365 --> 00:02:58,909
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's just to illustrate the point that, eh...

25
00:03:17,802 --> 00:03:22,705
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not free from sensuality, your mind is fully within the being of sensuality.

26
00:03:23,425 --> 00:03:26,707
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean being of chasing the pleasures wildly.

27
00:03:26,727 --> 00:03:26,827
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

28
00:03:26,847 --> 00:03:30,329
[SPEAKER_01]: It just means being within sense objects.

29
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:31,450
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your basis.

30
00:03:31,530 --> 00:03:32,251
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your world.

31
00:03:32,671 --> 00:03:33,511
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your extent.

32
00:03:34,352 --> 00:03:42,777
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point of view, it is impossible for you to experience any form of emotion, any form of feeling, that it will not be rooted

33
00:03:45,065 --> 00:03:53,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Within, upon, that very threshold, that very sense-object basis.

34
00:03:55,488 --> 00:04:03,030
[SPEAKER_01]: So, practically speaking, that means any disturbance, and I single out disturbance, because that's the whole point of practice.

35
00:04:03,771 --> 00:04:07,312
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not to get the pleasure, but it's to free yourself from suffering, which is the ultimate pleasure.

36
00:04:08,492 --> 00:04:11,393
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any form of disturbance, any form of suffering,

37
00:04:12,656 --> 00:04:19,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it possible to experience it, unless it's within sense or sense-object, or both?

38
00:04:20,841 --> 00:04:24,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless it's because of something on that domain.

39
00:04:25,417 --> 00:04:37,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Any form of disturbance, even if it's just mental idea of something, it's always going to be, well, boiled down to something you either see, hear, smell, taste, touch, or something you would have to see, hear, smell, taste, touch.

40
00:04:38,486 --> 00:04:52,835
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you think about it, when you look at the content of those thoughts, those thoughts, they are now, like, causing a disturbance, is because they imply something disagreeable of seeing, smelling, hearing, tasting, or touching.

41
00:04:54,273 --> 00:04:59,514
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even the thoughts are within that central domain, and the content of them is still the senses.

42
00:05:02,635 --> 00:05:11,937
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why that is, this is practically relevant, is because, well, the disturbance, any emotional discomfort, always feels like much more.

43
00:05:14,118 --> 00:05:18,179
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to actually start seeing it correctly as... also, it's just the sight.

44
00:05:19,956 --> 00:05:21,036
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm resisting here.

45
00:05:21,336 --> 00:05:23,077
[SPEAKER_00]: It always feels much more.

46
00:05:23,097 --> 00:05:28,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it always feels much more, because you always want to... well, you're always trying to get rid of it at face value.

47
00:05:29,258 --> 00:05:31,739
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you basically imply there is more to it than there is.

48
00:05:32,919 --> 00:05:42,761
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why for an arahant, who is fully freed from any craving, any resistance to anything that arises on the level of feeling, in the scene, there is just the scene.

49
00:05:43,381 --> 00:05:44,621
[SPEAKER_01]: In the herd, it's just the herd.

50
00:05:44,701 --> 00:05:46,102
[SPEAKER_01]: There isn't anything else there.

51
00:05:46,642 --> 00:05:47,582
[SPEAKER_01]: And there never was.

52
00:05:50,669 --> 00:05:52,230
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the pain, there's just the pain?

53
00:05:52,450 --> 00:05:56,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, in the... well, again, in the pain, there is just the pain, sure, from the point of your feeling.

54
00:05:56,854 --> 00:06:02,298
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to see, that pain is rooted upon something of the sense kind.

55
00:06:02,939 --> 00:06:04,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Sight, sound, smell, taste, touch.

56
00:06:04,380 --> 00:06:05,360
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be.

57
00:06:05,380 --> 00:06:08,963
[SPEAKER_01]: See, say, you're experiencing disturbance on account of somebody died.

58
00:06:09,744 --> 00:06:12,486
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, you will never see them again.

59
00:06:14,007 --> 00:06:16,189
[SPEAKER_01]: So, change in the domain of sight.

60
00:06:17,430 --> 00:06:18,631
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll never talk to them again.

61
00:06:19,350 --> 00:06:20,751
[SPEAKER_01]: You never get to touch them again.

62
00:06:21,872 --> 00:06:28,177
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's basically just deprived deprivation of the senses.

63
00:06:28,257 --> 00:06:31,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, certain things have changed now and you cannot experience in the same way.

64
00:06:33,361 --> 00:06:37,684
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that form that you used to see of that person now cannot be seen.

65
00:06:37,704 --> 00:06:40,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on account of that sight that has changed, you grieve.

66
00:06:41,627 --> 00:06:42,308
[SPEAKER_01]: You're disturbed.

67
00:06:45,590 --> 00:06:46,491
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is just the sight.

68
00:06:47,652 --> 00:06:49,013
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and always was the sight.

69
00:06:50,069 --> 00:06:56,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be able to experience love, infatuation, attachment, whatever, towards anyone, if you've never seen them?

70
00:06:57,973 --> 00:06:58,974
[SPEAKER_01]: If you've never heard them?

71
00:07:00,134 --> 00:07:01,055
[SPEAKER_01]: If you've never touched them?

72
00:07:02,676 --> 00:07:04,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be able to even think about it?

73
00:07:04,257 --> 00:07:04,417
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

74
00:07:04,497 --> 00:07:06,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

75
00:07:06,698 --> 00:07:11,200
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said that in many other suttas, just in slightly different contexts, but there was always a point.

76
00:07:11,220 --> 00:07:13,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have...

77
00:07:13,762 --> 00:07:17,564
[SPEAKER_01]: He asked that man, can you experience lust towards things that were not seen, heard,

78
00:07:18,105 --> 00:07:20,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, not basically experience through senses.

79
00:07:20,368 --> 00:07:23,431
[SPEAKER_01]: You say, no, it's impossible to experience lust, those things that I've never seen.

80
00:07:24,412 --> 00:07:30,579
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you realize, ah, so things you've never seen, you can't experience lust, which means things you've seen, your lust is fully circumstantial.

81
00:07:32,021 --> 00:07:35,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you've seen them, but you could have not seen them, and you will not see them.

82
00:07:37,296 --> 00:07:45,322
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not just that kind of puts the objects of your senses in perspective, but puts your own senses back into perspective as well.

83
00:07:45,963 --> 00:07:50,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, my own senses are disintegrating slowly, failing.

84
00:07:50,926 --> 00:07:54,429
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if sense objects are not changing, well, the senses will change.

85
00:07:55,470 --> 00:07:56,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way.

86
00:07:56,811 --> 00:08:02,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Not both can remain unchanged for the duration of your lifetime, let alone longer than that.

87
00:08:03,116 --> 00:08:04,236
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, this will last forever.

88
00:08:04,277 --> 00:08:04,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

89
00:08:07,252 --> 00:08:09,793
[SPEAKER_00]: And you experience change constantly, on a daily basis.

90
00:08:10,413 --> 00:08:12,313
[SPEAKER_01]: But you just keep focusing on the pleasant change.

91
00:08:14,974 --> 00:08:16,994
[SPEAKER_01]: When you think about change, ah...

92
00:08:17,034 --> 00:08:20,215
[SPEAKER_01]: But, whenever you suffer, you suffer on account of unpleasant change.

93
00:08:20,975 --> 00:08:21,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, change is there.

94
00:08:25,416 --> 00:08:25,936
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you...

95
00:08:32,597 --> 00:08:35,118
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are longing for a sight...

96
00:08:37,408 --> 00:08:40,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you say to yourself, well, that's just a sight?

97
00:08:41,871 --> 00:08:44,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you want to ask yourself, why?

98
00:08:45,273 --> 00:08:46,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you saying that to yourself now?

99
00:08:47,274 --> 00:08:49,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I'm longing for it, I'm suffering.

100
00:08:49,655 --> 00:08:50,996
[SPEAKER_01]: So longing is unpleasant.

101
00:08:51,376 --> 00:08:54,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So are you saying, that's just a sight, so you don't experience displeasure?

102
00:08:54,898 --> 00:08:55,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

103
00:08:55,579 --> 00:08:58,060
[SPEAKER_00]: So that I can force that idea into my mind.

104
00:08:58,080 --> 00:08:58,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

105
00:08:58,501 --> 00:09:03,684
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but that's... you can do literally anything from any sutta, if you do it like that, you'll be doing it wrongly.

106
00:09:03,764 --> 00:09:05,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Although you're doing what the sutta says,

107
00:09:06,065 --> 00:09:08,607
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not doing it rightly, so you will not get the benefit from it.

108
00:09:09,047 --> 00:09:10,809
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you shouldn't be trying to get rid of it.

109
00:09:11,329 --> 00:09:19,075
[SPEAKER_01]: There is an experience, the threshold, is you are emotionally engaged with the domain of sight.

110
00:09:19,976 --> 00:09:20,776
[SPEAKER_00]: You're longing for it.

111
00:09:21,117 --> 00:09:23,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Longing for it, wanting it, not wanting it.

112
00:09:23,459 --> 00:09:27,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you and the sight, there is a relationship there, on account of which you feel.

113
00:09:28,407 --> 00:09:31,750
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to accept that as a starting point.

114
00:09:31,770 --> 00:09:33,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, oh, I must get rid of this, I must deal with this.

115
00:09:34,031 --> 00:09:35,092
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

116
00:09:35,132 --> 00:09:36,193
[SPEAKER_01]: That has arisen on its own.

117
00:09:36,654 --> 00:09:38,675
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only thing you must do is understand it.

118
00:09:40,437 --> 00:09:44,500
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say understand it, I don't mean understand some hypothesis that arises on account of it.

119
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:46,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Understand the nature of that relationship.

120
00:09:47,242 --> 00:09:49,324
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature of longing for a sight?

121
00:09:50,124 --> 00:09:53,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it implies the necessity of your sense bases, as in your eyes.

122
00:09:53,787 --> 00:09:56,049
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it implies the presence of your eyes.

123
00:09:57,108 --> 00:10:02,711
[SPEAKER_01]: That pressure to go and see something you want, or the pressure to avoid seeing something you don't want.

124
00:10:05,172 --> 00:10:09,435
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't have the eyes, none of that's applicable anymore.

125
00:10:11,376 --> 00:10:18,360
[SPEAKER_01]: So, part of the understanding of that relationship is understanding the gratuity of eyes.

126
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:19,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Eyes see.

127
00:10:19,961 --> 00:10:21,421
[SPEAKER_01]: You're subjected to what they see.

128
00:10:22,822 --> 00:10:24,243
[SPEAKER_00]: But do you have control over your eyes?

129
00:10:25,411 --> 00:10:27,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Remind yourself of that context of...

130
00:10:27,613 --> 00:10:34,679
[SPEAKER_01]: These eyes are these lumpy flesh, balls of flesh in the world, like all these other random things, on account of which I get sights.

131
00:10:35,259 --> 00:10:35,599
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

132
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:36,720
[SPEAKER_01]: But I have no...

133
00:10:37,781 --> 00:10:41,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, I have no control over my eyes in a sense of...

134
00:10:41,364 --> 00:10:42,265
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not their owner.

135
00:10:43,315 --> 00:10:47,338
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't prevent them from suddenly not working anymore.

136
00:10:47,618 --> 00:10:48,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Or seeing something.

137
00:10:48,519 --> 00:10:50,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Or seeing something I don't want to see, exactly.

138
00:10:50,761 --> 00:10:53,203
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if you were truly a master, you would only see things you want to see.

139
00:10:53,963 --> 00:10:57,306
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be the master of that which arises on account of the eyes, which is sights.

140
00:10:57,446 --> 00:11:00,068
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, here you are, grieving on account of sights.

141
00:11:01,569 --> 00:11:10,836
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, when we talk about understanding, it's not about discerning some theoretical relationship and meaning, or secret.

142
00:11:11,356 --> 00:11:11,937
[SPEAKER_01]: It's literally

143
00:11:13,113 --> 00:11:18,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Drilling in, repetitively, the obviousness of the nature of these things.

144
00:11:19,118 --> 00:11:23,462
[SPEAKER_01]: But, I say the obviousness, because it's obvious, and because it's obvious, people don't think about it.

145
00:11:24,643 --> 00:11:27,346
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not enough to see that it's obvious that eyes are not yours.

146
00:11:27,706 --> 00:11:34,112
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to now keep repeat that same obvious seeing, well, for as long as you have lust in regard to sights left.

147
00:11:34,845 --> 00:11:40,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, if you understood that correctly, it's impossible to experience lust towards sights.

148
00:11:41,170 --> 00:11:41,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Or disturbance.

149
00:11:42,371 --> 00:11:43,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Any form of disturbance, yeah.

150
00:11:44,192 --> 00:11:50,037
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's because you don't see the nature of your senses... Because you haven't understood it, that's why you're still disturbed.

151
00:11:50,117 --> 00:11:55,321
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the obviousness of it should not fool you into thinking, okay, I got this, what next?

152
00:11:56,763 --> 00:11:57,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I got it.

153
00:11:57,623 --> 00:11:59,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Does that mean I'm free from sensuality?

154
00:12:00,164 --> 00:12:02,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Can a sight disturb me in the future?

155
00:12:02,086 --> 00:12:02,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

156
00:12:02,346 --> 00:12:03,407
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to keep reflecting.

157
00:12:03,508 --> 00:12:04,949
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the Buddha would always say.

158
00:12:05,670 --> 00:12:12,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, you practice, you have insights, things start making sense, but that's only part of the picture.

159
00:12:12,157 --> 00:12:17,943
[SPEAKER_01]: The other part is, you keep asking yourself, okay, so can unarisen sensuality arise now in me?

160
00:12:19,986 --> 00:12:24,329
[SPEAKER_01]: Does the reason sensuality last less, less, like, like, for a short amount of time?

161
00:12:24,790 --> 00:12:26,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Is my mind quicker to free?

162
00:12:26,411 --> 00:12:33,036
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all of these things you reflect to see, oh, am I actually free or is just kind of a temporary I made sense out of anything and I got a relief?

163
00:12:35,297 --> 00:12:37,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that experience.

164
00:12:37,199 --> 00:12:41,942
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to, you want that understanding to also prevent you from being disturbed in the future.

165
00:12:42,122 --> 00:12:45,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just freeing you from whatever was disturbing you while you were thinking about it.

166
00:12:48,147 --> 00:12:49,488
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I want, like, your eyes

167
00:12:50,634 --> 00:12:53,815
[SPEAKER_00]: What you see, do not disturb me.

168
00:12:55,956 --> 00:12:57,376
[SPEAKER_01]: You mean, my eyes do not disturb you.

169
00:12:57,396 --> 00:12:57,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

170
00:12:58,116 --> 00:12:58,596
[SPEAKER_00]: So, my eyes.

171
00:12:58,737 --> 00:12:58,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

172
00:12:59,437 --> 00:13:04,398
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is that... but understanding my eyes, in that same way.

173
00:13:04,538 --> 00:13:05,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

174
00:13:05,058 --> 00:13:08,880
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to start seeing your eyes, pretty much like other people's eyes.

175
00:13:09,200 --> 00:13:09,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a stranger's.

176
00:13:09,840 --> 00:13:14,321
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, your eyes is the closest to your point of view, than other people's eyes.

177
00:13:14,581 --> 00:13:16,402
[SPEAKER_01]: But either way, eyes are there, sights are there.

178
00:13:20,075 --> 00:13:24,056
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, your eyes are not more yours than my eyes are.

179
00:13:25,356 --> 00:13:26,656
[SPEAKER_00]: They're just closer.

180
00:13:26,936 --> 00:13:30,457
[SPEAKER_01]: They're closer in their arrangement, whichever way you experience the world.

181
00:13:30,517 --> 00:13:34,458
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that proximity, oh, this is mine.

182
00:13:34,978 --> 00:13:36,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's nothing else between me and this.

183
00:13:37,298 --> 00:13:42,960
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a lot in between me and your eyes, but between me and my eyes, there's nothing in between.

184
00:13:43,700 --> 00:13:44,420
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just I see.

185
00:13:45,518 --> 00:13:52,122
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the sense bases are always described in that kind of negative terms, like as absent, so to speak.

186
00:13:52,282 --> 00:13:53,583
[SPEAKER_01]: They're defined by things you see.

187
00:13:53,903 --> 00:13:57,886
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't see your eyes, but the eyes are there because of which you see.

188
00:13:58,266 --> 00:14:06,151
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't hear your ears, because there's nothing, nothing in between you, nothing that you can perceive between you and your sense bases.

189
